WEBVTT

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:10.526
We will manage a small game-state file and compare its

00:00:10.526 --> 00:00:11.946
text at two moments.

00:00:11.946 --> 00:00:15.214
Every file belongs to a newly created practice

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:15.924
directory.

00:00:15.924 --> 00:00:19.547
This keeps copying, moving, and deleting focused on

00:00:19.547 --> 00:00:21.465
files made by this example.

00:01:10.451 --> 00:01:14.065
A copy creates another file while keeping the source.

00:01:14.065 --> 00:01:16.520
A move changes its location or name.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.906
Our destination paths start unused.

00:01:18.906 --> 00:01:22.384
Existence and regular-file checks describe what the

00:01:22.384 --> 00:01:24.020
program can observe now.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.862
A regular file stores data.

00:01:25.862 --> 00:01:27.566
A directory groups files.

00:01:27.566 --> 00:01:31.044
A false check can also mean the status could not be

00:01:31.044 --> 00:01:31.794
determined.

00:01:31.794 --> 00:01:34.726
The catch handles input or output failures.

00:01:35.034 --> 00:01:38.237
Metadata is information about a file, such as its

00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:39.348
stored byte size.

00:01:39.348 --> 00:01:42.813
A content snapshot keeps text from one read for later

00:01:42.813 --> 00:01:43.532
comparison.

00:01:43.532 --> 00:01:46.931
Here, previous saves the first text and previousSize

00:01:46.931 --> 00:01:48.304
saves its byte count.

00:01:48.304 --> 00:01:51.507
After writing again, current saves a second read.

00:01:51.507 --> 00:01:55.037
Comparing the strings asks whether their text differs.

00:01:55.037 --> 00:01:56.606
Deleting removes a file.

00:01:56.606 --> 00:01:59.613
deleteIfExists reports whether it removed one.

00:01:59.613 --> 00:02:02.359
A directory must be empty before deletion.

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:04.637
Predict all seven output lines.

00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:08.473
Track the three file paths, compare the saved text and

00:02:08.473 --> 00:02:11.884
sizes, then consider both attempts to delete the

00:02:11.884 --> 00:02:12.452
archive.

00:02:12.452 --> 00:02:13.234
Pause here.

00:02:18.087 --> 00:02:21.581
The source remains, the backup name disappears after

00:02:21.581 --> 00:02:24.538
the move, and the archive is a regular file.

00:02:24.538 --> 00:02:28.032
Both stored texts occupy six bytes in UTF eight, but

00:02:28.032 --> 00:02:29.510
their contents differ.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.930
The first archive deletion succeeds.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.886
The second reports that nothing was deleted.

00:02:34.886 --> 00:02:38.313
Finally, the program removes its state file and the

00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:40.262
now-empty practice directory.

00:02:40.589 --> 00:02:43.299
Polling means checking at separate moments.

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:46.386
These two reads notice a difference between their

00:02:46.386 --> 00:02:47.016
snapshots.

00:02:47.016 --> 00:02:50.104
Change only the second written text back to ready

00:02:50.104 --> 00:02:51.616
followed by its newline.

00:02:51.616 --> 00:02:54.641
Predict both comparison results, then run again.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:57.854
Could a change from ready to another value and back

00:02:57.854 --> 00:03:00.564
entirely between reads escape these checks?
